  "worked_solution": "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8{e}^{t+1},\\,12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)\u27e9\\text{d}t=\u27e8\\int {e}^{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)={e}^{t+1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t+1}\\text{d}t={e}^{t+1}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t),$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=3t.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u)+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e8{e}^{t+1},\\,12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)\u27e9\\text{d}t & =\u27e8{e}^{t+1}+{c}_{1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)+{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{e}^{t+1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)\u27e9+\u27e8{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{e}^{t+1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)\u27e9+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
